WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:05.120
Where did Boba Fett get his fly armor? He was&nbsp;
originally supposed to be Anakin's brother?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:09.040
Eaten by a Sarlaac TWICE? See how&nbsp;
the baddest bounty hunter in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:11.520
galaxy became the Boba Fett that we know and love.

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:16.800
It may be hard for Fett fanatics to fathom, but&nbsp;
George Lucas had no idea that Boba Fett would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:20.880
become a fan-favorite. On the "Return&nbsp;
of the Jedi" commentary track, he said

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:25.440
"In the case of Boba Fett's death, had I known&nbsp;
he was gonna turn into such a popular character,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:29.600
I probably would've made it a little bit&nbsp;
more exciting. He became such a favorite of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:33.200
everybody's that, for having such a&nbsp;
small part, he had a very large presence.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:36.720
And now that his history has been told&nbsp;
in the first trilogy, it makes it even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:40.320
more of a misstep that we wouldn't make&nbsp;
more out of the event of his defeat."

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:45.440
It's an interesting admission from Lucas, because&nbsp;
it answers the age-old question of why such a cool&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:51.040
character experienced such an ignominious end. The&nbsp;
short answer: to Lucas, Fett’s death deserved as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:55.200
little attention as any of the other thugs and&nbsp;
aliens that Luke and his friends dispatched.

00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:57.440
"Return of the Jedi" editor Duwayne Dunham,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.440 --> 00:01:01.920
who was the first person to wear the&nbsp;
Boba Fett armor in a 1978 screen test,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:07.600
said in 2014 that he and others tried to talk&nbsp;
Lucas out of killing Fett in such a dumb way.

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:11.040
"We kinda mounted a protest saying&nbsp;
'You can't do that to Boba Fett!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:13.760
He's deserving of more!' But they threw him in."

00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:16.800
Lucas also admitted on the "Return&nbsp;
of the Jedi" commentary track that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:20.160
he almost added a new scene during&nbsp;
the Special Edition revamps in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:24.400
1990's which would have seen Fett&nbsp;
emerging alive from the Sarlacc Pit.

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:28.080
"I'd contemplated putting in that extra&nbsp;
shot where he climbs out of the hole,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:30.880
but y'know I figure that's [...]&nbsp;
it doesn't quite fit, in the end."

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:34.480
Ultimately, fans are probably thankful. After all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.480 --> 00:01:37.760
Fett's re-emergence in "Mandalorian"&nbsp;
wouldn't have been nearly as effective&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.760 --> 00:01:41.600
if fans had already seen him climb out&nbsp;
of the Sarlacc's mouth in the late '90s.

00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:42.320
"You were dead."

00:01:43.120 --> 00:01:45.520
"She was left for dead on the sands on Tatooine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.480 --> 00:01:50.960
as was I. But fate sometimes&nbsp;
steps in to rescue the wretched."

00:01:51.520 --> 00:01:55.840
Of course, by the time the "Return of the&nbsp;
Jedi" Special Edition came out in 1997,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.840 --> 00:01:59.360
fans had already seen Boba Fett&nbsp;
emerge from the Sarlacc Pit alive.

00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:02.000
The only problem is that he&nbsp;
immediately fell back in it.

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:06.640
The story happened in the officially licensed&nbsp;
"Star Wars" comic book series from Marvel Comics.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.640 --> 00:02:11.920
In "Star Wars" #81, which is the first story to&nbsp;
take place after the end of "Return of the Jedi,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:17.520
Fett escapes by being violently burped up. Injured&nbsp;
and dazed, some nearby Jawas pick him up and put&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:23.280
him inside their massive Sandcrawler. Inside&nbsp;
is also R2-D2, whom Han Solo, Leia, and Luke&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:28.240
eventually rescue from the speeding crawler. At&nbsp;
one point, Han finds Fett and tries to help him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.240 --> 00:02:33.120
but Fett attacks and is left behind as the&nbsp;
Sandcrawler speeds right into the Sarlacc Pit.

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:35.840
So why did this happen? According to some sources,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.840 --> 00:02:40.400
writer Jo Duffy desperately wanted to use&nbsp;
Boba Fett, but the only way LucasFilm would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:44.160
allow it was if she put him right back into&nbsp;
the Pit when she was done with him. Bummer.

00:02:44.720 --> 00:02:49.280
It's somewhat ironic that George Lucas decided&nbsp;
not to add in new scenes of Boba Fett escaping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.280 --> 00:02:54.000
the Sarlacc PIt because he thought it would be out&nbsp;
of place, as many fans feel the scenes he did add&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:58.400
with Boba Fett are among the most contrived&nbsp;
additions to the entire revamped trilogy.

00:02:58.400 --> 00:03:02.240
And that's saying a lot, because while some of&nbsp;
the changes in the Special Edition were simple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.240 --> 00:03:06.640
effects tweaks done to enhance the film with&nbsp;
new technology, or restoring lost footage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.640 --> 00:03:10.800
others changed important character&nbsp;
moments, most famously by having Greedo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:14.880
shoot first instead of Han during their&nbsp;
confrontation at the Mos Eisley cantina.

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:18.880
The first new Fett footage was added to&nbsp;
"A New Hope" during the cantina sequence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.880 --> 00:03:23.200
where Han runs into Jabba the Hutt shortly&nbsp;
after shooting Greedo. The scene was filmed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.200 --> 00:03:27.360
way back in the 70s for the original film,&nbsp;
but had been cut because the effects for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:31.360
Jabba weren't good enough. When it was&nbsp;
restored in the 90's with CGI, though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.360 --> 00:03:36.400
in addition to Jabba they also added in Boba&nbsp;
Fett just as a sort of Easter Egg for fans.

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:40.560
It's his appearance in "Return of the Jedi,"&nbsp;
though, that some Fett fanatics despise.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:42.800
Lucas added a short bit with Fett leering at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:46.720
Jabba's dancers and playfully touching&nbsp;
a dancer on the chin as he walks out.

00:03:46.720 --> 00:03:50.720
What's wrong with this, you ask? A large&nbsp;
part of what made Boba Fett scary was that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.720 --> 00:03:54.800
he seemed to have no human weaknesses&nbsp;
or vices, just a relentless dedication&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.800 --> 00:03:59.440
to his goal. These Special Edition tweaks,&nbsp;
however, present him just like any other guy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.440 --> 00:04:03.920
distracted by the beautiful women around him.&nbsp;
While this does open the door for fan theories&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.920 --> 00:04:08.080
about his getting knocked into the Sarlacc&nbsp;
because he was distracted by Leia's gold bikini,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.080 --> 00:04:12.640
it also reveals that years after Lucas was first&nbsp;
surprised by the fan love for the character,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.640 --> 00:04:17.040
he still didn't understand what made the aloof&nbsp;
bounty hunter so cool in the first place.

00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:21.760
You can tell by looking at Fett that he's&nbsp;
pretty well-armored and carries a lot of gear.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.760 --> 00:04:26.240
His go-to weapon is an EE-3 carbine&nbsp;
rifle. Also close at hand are a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.240 --> 00:04:30.320
disruptor pistol and a concussion grenade&nbsp;
launcher. Then there's the flamethrower,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.320 --> 00:04:36.320
dart launcher, fiber-cord wrist launcher gauntlet,&nbsp;
vibro-blades, concussive rocket, and a jetpack.

00:04:36.320 --> 00:04:41.680
Fett's helmet also allows for full 360-degree&nbsp;
vision. It interfaces with his ship's computer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.680 --> 00:04:46.160
provides water, can seal against changes&nbsp;
in atmospheric pressure, record video,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.160 --> 00:04:48.960
and amplify sound. The armor itself is designed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:52.960
resist the elements and minimize impact&nbsp;
from anything including blaster fire,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.960 --> 00:04:57.200
while the gauntlets are decked out with a host&nbsp;
of different types of rockets — most of which are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.200 --> 00:05:02.080
meant to stun rather than kill, since a bounty&nbsp;
hunter can't get paid if he blows up his prey.

00:05:02.080 --> 00:05:05.600
Of course, most of this never made&nbsp;
it onto film and, realistically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.600 --> 00:05:08.800
Fett did almost nothing in the original&nbsp;
trilogy beyond looking pretty cool and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.800 --> 00:05:12.720
then selling Han Solo down the river to&nbsp;
Jabba. But it was a love of the character's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.720 --> 00:05:17.360
potential that inspired later "Star Wars"&nbsp;
writers to flesh him out in other projects.

00:05:17.360 --> 00:05:21.360
Besides his iconic armor, Boba Fett&nbsp;
also flies a very specific ship,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.360 --> 00:05:25.120
which boasts a different design from&nbsp;
other ships in the "Star Wars" universe.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.120 --> 00:05:29.040
And it also used to boast a very&nbsp;
specific name as well: Slave-1.

00:05:29.040 --> 00:05:33.680
Though the ship is never called Slave-1 by name in&nbsp;
any of the live action films, the designation was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.680 --> 00:05:39.520
used in Extended Universe projects like toys,&nbsp;
video games, comics, and cartoons for decades.

00:05:39.520 --> 00:05:44.800
However, in 2021 the new LEGO Star Wars&nbsp;
Mandalorian sets came out, and the ship was just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.800 --> 00:05:49.840
called "Boba Fett's Starship." In an interview&nbsp;
with Jedi news, the LEGO Star Wars designers said&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.840 --> 00:05:54.080
that the name Slave-1, quote, "is just something&nbsp;
that Disney doesn't want to use anymore."

00:05:54.080 --> 00:05:59.040
Of course, some fans were internet-outraged, but&nbsp;
by any name, it's still a cool ship. "Boba Fett's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.040 --> 00:06:04.560
Starship" is a Republic-era Firespray-31-class&nbsp;
spacecraft previously owned by Fett's father,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.560 --> 00:06:09.200
Jango Fett, during the Clone Wars. Like Fett's&nbsp;
portable armory of tools and weapons on his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.200 --> 00:06:14.080
person, the "Starship" is highly-customized and&nbsp;
armed to the teeth with both subtle and striking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.080 --> 00:06:19.440
weaponry. One of the most unique features of the&nbsp;
"Starship" is its ability to rotate on a gimbal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.440 --> 00:06:23.120
flying vertically in the air and space&nbsp;
while landing horizontally — all while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.120 --> 00:06:25.920
keeping the pilot's seat and&nbsp;
cargo area facing upright.

00:06:26.560 --> 00:06:28.640
There are many different languages in Star Wars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.640 --> 00:06:33.200
and a special code shared between Boba Fett&nbsp;
and his father, Jango Fett, is one of them.

00:06:33.200 --> 00:06:38.480
First identified as a specific language in a&nbsp;
2019 video on the Star Wars Kids YouTube channel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.480 --> 00:06:42.880
titled "Every Language in Star Wars,"&nbsp;
Fett Code is briefly heard in "Episode II:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.880 --> 00:06:46.800
Attack of the Clones," when Obi-Wan Kenobi&nbsp;
meets the two at their home on Kamino.

00:06:46.800 --> 00:06:50.800
Jango speaks to his son in Fett Code as&nbsp;
Obi-Wan questions the bounty hunter about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.800 --> 00:06:55.600
any recent trips to Coruscant and his feelings&nbsp;
about the clone army created in his image.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.600 --> 00:06:59.440
It's not clear exactly what Jango tells&nbsp;
his son in Fett Code, but it's clear it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.440 --> 00:07:03.440
was instruction to close a nearby closet&nbsp;
containing Jango's Mandalorian armor.

00:07:03.440 --> 00:07:05.600
[speaking in Fett Code]

00:07:05.600 --> 00:07:08.000
The code is never heard or spoken of again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.000 --> 00:07:12.480
implying that Fett Code was probably created&nbsp;
by Jango to privately converse with his son.

00:07:13.120 --> 00:07:16.720
Boba Fett isn't a born and raised&nbsp;
Mandalorian, but that hasn't stopped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.720 --> 00:07:20.320
him from being the armored fighter everyone&nbsp;
thinks of when they think of Mandalorians.

00:07:20.320 --> 00:07:25.440
Fett is an exact clone of his father, Jango, who&nbsp;
was a foundling of the Mandalorians. Foundlings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.440 --> 00:07:30.320
were a key part of Mandalorian culture, in which&nbsp;
the planet's warriors adopted orphaned children&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.320 --> 00:07:35.280
and raised them as Mandalorians. Din Djarin in&nbsp;
"The Mandalorian" is, similarly, a foundling.

00:07:35.280 --> 00:07:39.840
Jango's traditional Mandalorian armor is passed&nbsp;
down to Fett, following the former bounty hunter's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.840 --> 00:07:44.240
death at the hands of Jedi Mace Windu. The&nbsp;
original armor was all-over silver Beskar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.240 --> 00:07:48.800
metal with the helmet featuring blue stripes&nbsp;
along the viewpoints. Fett later customized his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.800 --> 00:07:53.680
father's armor with new green and red paint and&nbsp;
added even more tools and weapons to his belt.

00:07:53.680 --> 00:07:56.640
In "The Mandalorian," Fett is&nbsp;
open about not being Mandalorian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.640 --> 00:07:59.600
but still wearing the armor because&nbsp;
it was given to him by his father.

00:07:59.600 --> 00:08:00.960
"Your father was a foundling?"

00:08:01.680 --> 00:08:05.360
"Yes. He even fought in the&nbsp;
Mandalorian civil wars."

00:08:06.960 --> 00:08:08.560
"Then that armor belongs to you."

00:08:08.560 --> 00:08:12.720
This strange anecdote actually came from&nbsp;
Luke Skywalker himself, Mark Hamill.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.720 --> 00:08:17.200
In Twitter exchanges in 2018, Hamill shared&nbsp;
that he once pitched to Star Wars creator&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.200 --> 00:08:21.600
George Lucas to have the Fett character be&nbsp;
Luke's real mother underneath the helmet.

00:08:21.600 --> 00:08:25.360
"I have always been more than willing to&nbsp;
generously share my many, many terrible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.360 --> 00:08:29.440
ideas in the off-chance we might stumble&nbsp;
across one that could actually be useful."

00:08:29.440 --> 00:08:34.400
Hamill also said the suggestion was, quote, "the&nbsp;
only way we could top Vader being my father" and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.400 --> 00:08:38.960
that he pictured the character "as a double-agent&nbsp;
working clandestinely for the Rebels."

00:08:38.960 --> 00:08:42.080
Boba Fett may not have been revealed&nbsp;
as Luke Skywalker's mother, but the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.080 --> 00:08:45.840
bounty hunter was almost written&nbsp;
as Anakin Skywalker's stepbrother.

00:08:45.840 --> 00:08:50.240
In the book "Skywalking: The Life and Films&nbsp;
of George Lucas," it is revealed that Lucas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.240 --> 00:08:55.600
considered making Fett and Anakin related.&nbsp;
Though the biography came out in 1983,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.600 --> 00:09:00.960
Lucas was reportedly already considering ideas for&nbsp;
prequel films to his original trilogy. According&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.960 --> 00:09:06.960
to Lucas's ex-wife Marcia, quote, "George had Boba&nbsp;
Fett and Vader being brothers in the prequels."

00:09:06.960 --> 00:09:11.520
But the idea was ultimately deemed silly&nbsp;
and scrapped. Anakin Skywalker did have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.520 --> 00:09:16.080
a stepbrother however, in Owen Lars.&nbsp;
Owen was the stepson of Shmi Skywalker,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.080 --> 00:09:20.240
Anakin's mother. Owen and his wife&nbsp;
Beru took a baby Luke Skywalker in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.240 --> 00:09:24.320
after the death of Padme Amidala and&nbsp;
Anakin's transition to the dark side.

00:09:24.880 --> 00:09:29.120
Before Disney bought Lucasfilm and subsequently&nbsp;
scrapped several Star Wars projects,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.120 --> 00:09:34.240
Boba Fett was planned as the main character&nbsp;
of the dark action game "Star Wars 1313,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.240 --> 00:09:37.920
and was also the intended subject&nbsp;
of a "Solo"-esque standalone film.

00:09:37.920 --> 00:09:43.200
The video game was set among the darker sides&nbsp;
of the galaxy, on Coruscant's Level 1313,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.200 --> 00:09:48.080
with players first assuming the role of an unnamed&nbsp;
bounty hunter and then playing as Fett. When the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.080 --> 00:09:53.520
game was introduced at E3 in 2012, creators said&nbsp;
players would use "exotic" weapons to help Fett&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.520 --> 00:09:58.400
investigate a criminal conspiracy in the darkest,&nbsp;
lowest levels of the planet-wide metropolis.

00:09:58.400 --> 00:10:01.920
A more recent Fett-focused offering&nbsp;
was the reported standalone film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.920 --> 00:10:07.360
similar to "Solo." Though the project was never&nbsp;
formally announced, rumors swirled in 2013 and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.360 --> 00:10:13.120
again in 2018 that Disney and Lucasfilm had been&nbsp;
working on a Boba Fett movie with director James&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.120 --> 00:10:18.000
Mangold. But later that year, entertainment&nbsp;
outlets confirmed the Fett movie was no more.

00:10:18.000 --> 00:10:22.160
Around the same time, Disney and Lucasfilm&nbsp;
began heavily promoting the space western&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.160 --> 00:10:26.560
series "The Mandalorian" on Disney+,&nbsp;
so it can likely be assumed that plot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.560 --> 00:10:30.640
points/characters in that Fett film either&nbsp;
overlapped with the "Mandalorian" mythology&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.640 --> 00:10:35.120
or were perceived to potentially contradict&nbsp;
it. At the end of the day, Fett's resurrection&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.120 --> 00:10:39.600
would come solely via "The Mandalorian," and&nbsp;
will continue with "The Book of Boba Fett."

